  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Vocal abierta o cerrada",
    "@en": "Open or closed voice"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**É** y **ó** suelen representar vocales abiertas, como en **pé** y **avó**. **Ê** y **ô** representan vocales cerradas, como en **avô** y **pôr**. El acento grave de **à** tiene otra función: marca una contracción.",
     "@en": "**É** and **ó** usually represent open vowels, as in **pé** and **avó**. **Ê** and **ô** represent closed vowels, as in **avô** and **pôr**. The severe accent of **à** has another function: it marks a contraction."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Compara **avó** (abuela) y **avô** (abuelo); **pode** (puede) y **pôde** (pudo); **por** (por) y **pôr** (poner).",
     "@en": "Compare **avó** (grandmother) and **avô** (grandfather); **pode** (may) and **pôde** (could); **por** (per) and **pôr** (put)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "La vocal átona",
    "@en": "The Atone Vowel"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "En el portugués europeo, algunas vocales átonas se reducen mucho al pronunciarse. Esta variación de pronunciación no cambia la ortografía de la palabra.",
     "@en": "In European Portuguese, some atone vocals are greatly reduced by their pronouncement. This variation in pronunciation does not change the spelling of the word."
    }
   ]
